Working title:

When Method Becomes Contamination: AI-Use Stigma and Reflexive Othering in a Sociology Forum

Core thesis:

A public sociology discussion about chatbot transcript preservation became a live example of the problem it was asked to examine. The thread shifted from the methodological question, how to preserve full chatbot-use records, to the social sorting of the speaker. This shows how anti-AI boundary work can reproduce othering even among participants familiar with othering as a concept.

Paper shape:

Case
A software tester raised a methods issue: chatbot-use research needs full transcripts because screenshots, memory, and fragments create weak records.
Expected response
A sociology-oriented forum might be expected to notice methodology, workflow evidence, platform power, participant records, and data custody.
Observed response
The thread largely treated AI-use keywords as contamination markers. The speaker’s role was repeatedly misread or challenged before the method issue was engaged.
Mechanism
The complaint moved from “the research record is being weakened” to “the speaker/post is not acceptable.” That is object substitution.
Othering pattern
The speaker’s claim was treated as suspect because of group-coded signals: AI use, GitHub link, non-academic status, unusual phrasing, and outsider position.
Reflexive failure
When othering was named, the forum did not reliably pause to inspect its own sorting behaviour. Some users did eventually provide useful reframing, which becomes the counterexample.
Research value
This is not just Reddit drama. It is a small live case of how anti-AI identity formation may interfere with the study of AI use itself.
